  "details": "This opens up significant possibilities for world creation and development on VRChat. At present, platforms like Resonite already support WebSocket functionality (Source: https://wiki.resonite.com/Component:WebsocketClient ), so there is no technical reason why VRChat couldn't implement this as well. Integrating WebSockets would enhance the overall gaming experience on VRChat by enabling better data caching and reducing reliance on tools like VRCStringDownload.\n\nWhile I understand this would increase the complexity and scope for developing VRChat worlds, the potential benefits outweigh the additional effort.\n\nKey Advantages of This Approach:\n\n1. Enhanced Connectivity Between Instances: VRChat worlds could become more interconnected, especially in MMO-scale games. For instance, consider a game set in space, similar to Star Citizen, where different instances could seamlessly interact.\n\n2. Optimized Data Processing with External Software: External applications could take over tasks where Udon scripting in VRChat falls short, especially by leveraging GPU Compute or SIMD-Optimized Assembly for data processing.\n\n3. Improved Caching and Distributed Data Streaming: This would be particularly useful for larger worlds that require efficient streaming and caching of data.\n\nPotential Use Case:\n\nConsider a scenario where players are sculpting in a VRChat world, similar to the functionality of Adobe Medium. Synchronizing sculpted data across multiple players becomes highly complex, especially given that not all players have powerful enough systems to process this information. Additionally, saving and preserving these creations presents a challenge, as the current system largely depends on VRCStringDownloader, which is limited by URL query sizes for uploading data (approximately 2 KB to 16 KB every 5 seconds, though this hasn’t been fully tested).\n\nWebSocket implementation would address many of these challenges:\n\n1. Efficient Data Synchronization: Data could be synchronized more effectively by utilizing a dedicated server.\n\n2. Offloading Complex Processing: The processing of complex data, such as 3D sculptures, could be offloaded to external applications that are optimized for GPU Compute.\n\n3. Reliable Data Storage: Players would have the ability to save and retrieve their work more efficiently, solving the limitations currently posed by URL-based data transfers.\n\nBusiness Value to VRChat:\n\n1. Expanding Creative Possibilities: By enabling WebSocket support, you expand what is possible in VRChat worlds. This would incentivize more players to participate in the creator economy, directly increasing revenue through user-generated content.\n\n2. Reducing CDN Costs: Offloading part of the network load to players, especially through peer-to-peer protocols, would alleviate VRChat’s CDN costs. Users would handle some of the traffic themselves, leading to potential savings on infrastructure.\n\n3. Closing the Feature Gap with Competitors: This move would bring VRChat closer to feature parity with platforms like Resonite, making it more competitive and appealing to users looking for advanced world-building features.\n\nPlease and thank you,\n\nOpa",
